pages.body:
Cannabis grade language can sound official because the same words appear across menus, stores and conversations.

AA.

AAA.

AAA+.

AAAA.

Quads.

Mids.

Premium.

Exotic.

Top shelf.

These words can help adults communicate quickly.

They do not come from one universal Canadian government grading system.

## AA Weed

AA is familiar retail shorthand.

At Jane Finch Cannabis, AA Weed is an established flower collection.

The label helps narrow the menu.

It does not guarantee one THC range, aroma profile, bud size or cultivation method.

## AAA and AAA+ Weed

AAA is another familiar cannabis retail term.

AAA+ suggests positioning slightly above basic AAA language.

At Jane Finch Cannabis, AAA+ Weed is its own established collection.

Use the current category for current products.

Use this guide for the terminology.

## Premium Weed

Premium is broad quality-positioning language.

A product may be positioned as Premium because of:

- genetics;
- producer;
- aroma;
- trim;
- cure;
- presentation;
- scarcity;
- current menu positioning.

The word still does not create one mandatory THC or quality formula.

## Exotic Weed

Exotic can suggest:

- unusual genetics;
- distinctive aroma;
- rarity;
- strong bag appeal;
- high-end positioning.

It is not an official government grade.

At Jane Finch Cannabis, Exotic Weed is a separate collection.

## Mids

Mids means cannabis someone considers middle-of-the-road.

The exact reason can vary.

The speaker may mean:

- average aroma;
- average presentation;
- ordinary trim;
- average value;
- simply “not top shelf.”

Because mids is subjective, ask what characteristic they mean.

## Top Shelf

Top shelf generally signals premium positioning.

It can overlap with Premium, Exotic or quads language, but the words are not exact synonyms.

The useful question is:

What characteristic is making someone call it top shelf?

## Quads / AAAA

Quads is common Canadian slang for AAAA.

It generally suggests premium positioning.

It is not tied to one mandatory THC percentage, bud size or terpene level.

## Grade Language Does Not Replace Product Information

A grade term does not replace:

- producer;
- cultivar;
- THC/CBD;
- package details;
- current price;
- current product notes.

Use the grade to narrow the browse.

Use the actual product information to understand the product.

## THC Is Not the Grade

Higher THC does not automatically mean a higher grade.

THC is one important measurement.

Grade language often includes things THC does not directly measure:

- aroma;
- trim;
- cure;
- freshness;
- bud structure;
- visible trichomes.

Craft sounds premium.

Commercial sounds large-scale.

Those impressions are common.

They are not complete definitions of quality.

The terms can describe:

- production scale;
- process;
- branding;
- batch size;
- producer identity;
- how hands-on an operation presents itself.

Neither word guarantees the finished flower.

## What People Usually Mean by Craft

Craft often suggests:

- smaller-scale production;
- close batch attention;
- hands-on cultivation;
- producer identity built around growing detail.

The word is broad.

It should not automatically be treated as one specific regulatory licence class unless that is actually confirmed for the producer.

## What People Usually Mean by Commercial

Commercial often points toward larger-scale production and distribution.

Larger operations may emphasize:

- standardization;
- repeatability;
- process control;
- consistency.

That does not automatically mean lower quality.

Scale can create strengths and trade-offs.

## Small Is Not Automatically Better

A small producer can make excellent flower.

A small producer can also produce an inconsistent batch.

A large producer can make average flower.

A large producer can also make carefully finished flower.

Scale alone does not settle the comparison.

## Craft Is Not the Same as Exotic or Premium

Craft describes production context.

Exotic and Premium are retail positioning terms.

They can overlap.

They are not synonyms.

A craft-labelled product does not automatically belong in the Exotic Weed collection.

An Exotic Weed product is not automatically craft.

## Compare the Product, Not Just the Producer Label

Adults may compare:

- producer;
- cultivar;
- legal THC/CBD information;
- aroma description where provided;
- trim;
- structure;
- visible trichomes;
- moisture/freshness clues;
- current category context.

Those details are more specific than the broad craft/commercial label.

Cannabis shoppers say strain all the time.

Growers and horticulture writers may prefer cultivar.

Both words can be useful.

The bigger lesson is that a familiar name does not guarantee every product carrying that name is genetically or chemically identical.

## Strain Is Normal Cannabis Language

“Strain” is deeply established in cannabis culture.

Adults understand it.

There is no need to correct ordinary customer language simply to sound technical.

## Cultivar Is a Horticultural Term

Cultivar is short for “cultivated variety.”

It is used in horticulture for plant lines selected and maintained for particular characteristics.

In cannabis education, it is useful because it keeps the conversation focused on the plant line rather than a promised effect.

## The Same Name Can Appear Across Different Producers

Different producers may work with:

- different cuts;
- different breeding lines;
- different selections;
- different cultivation conditions.

That means similarly named products can differ in:

- appearance;
- aroma;
- labelled chemistry;
- structure;
- finished quality.

The name helps identify the product.

It is not a DNA certificate.

## Growing and Post-Harvest Work Still Matter

The finished flower is influenced by:

- genetics;
- cultivation;
- plant health;
- harvest timing;
- drying;
- curing;
- storage.

A cultivar name is the beginning of the story, not the end.

## Do Not Use a Name as an Effect Guarantee

A famous strain or cultivar name does not guarantee one exact experience for every person.

Use actual product information.

Avoid turning the name into a medical or effect promise.

Cannabis genetics are often explained like a family tree.

Landrace sits closer to older regional roots.

Hybrid describes crossing and selection that shaped much of modern commercial cannabis.

Neither term is a quality grade.

## Landrace

Landrace generally refers to cannabis populations associated with long-term adaptation to particular geographic regions before modern commercial breeding mixed many lineages together.

The word points toward:

- regional history;
- local adaptation;
- older plant populations.

It does not mean every plant from a region is genetically identical or untouched by human selection.

## Hybrid

A hybrid is produced by crossing genetic lines.

Breeders may select plants for traits such as:

- structure;
- flowering time;
- aroma;
- cannabinoid profile;
- yield;
- resilience;
- appearance.

The resulting line can be selected and crossed again.

## Why Modern Cannabis Has Mixed Ancestry

Commercial breeding has been active for decades.

That helps explain why today's Indica/Sativa/Hybrid labels do not map cleanly to three separate genetic groups.

Many current cultivars contain several layers of ancestry.

## Landrace Does Not Mean Automatically Better

Older regional origin can be interesting.

It does not automatically mean:

- stronger;
- safer;
- more aromatic;
- more premium;
- better cured.

Hybrid does not mean diluted or lower quality.

Those are value judgements, not genetic definitions.

## Why This Matters on the Menu

Genetics can add context.

Adults still need the actual producer, label and current product information.
